// Reference query. Not deployed in any employer environment.
// Title: Defender for Identity Tier 0 credential-access alerts staged and chained per source entity (reconnaissance, Kerberoasting, certificate abuse, directory replication)
// Status: untested template
// ATT&CK: T1087.002 (Account Discovery: Domain Account), T1558.003 (Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets: Kerberoasting), T1649 (Steal or Forge Authentication Certificates), T1003.006 (OS Credential Dumping: DCSync); ATT&CK v19.2
// Data sources: Microsoft Defender XDR advanced hunting, AlertInfo and AlertEvidence (alerts raised by Microsoft Defender for Identity sensors on domain controllers, AD CS servers, and Entra Connect servers)
// Licensing assumptions: Microsoft Defender for Identity within the scenario's Defender XDR stack (Microsoft 365 E5), with sensors on every Tier 0 server as 03-identity-and-access.md section 4 requires
// Rule ID: DX-05 | Platform: Defender XDR advanced hunting, custom detection candidate at the every-12-hours or every-24-hours frequency, or a hunting query run on the same schedule (see Tuning knobs) | Suggested severity: Critical when a chain reaches replication
// Tactics: Discovery (TA0007), Credential Access (TA0006)
// Answers: detection-priorities.md items 6, 7, and 8 (Priority 2) and part of item 18 (Priority 3); attack-paths.md AP-4 step 6, AP-5 steps 2 to 5
// Architecture context: IDS-AD, PIP-PKI, PIP-ID-RISK (Defender for Identity), Z-T0; residual risks RR-02 and RR-07; risk register R-05 and R-08
// Fictional scenario: Contoso Regional Health (fictional). Reference portfolio content.
//
// Purpose
//   On the local clinical plane (RR-02) Defender for Identity carries most of the detection load. Each of
//   its alerts already stands alone; this query adds what a single alert cannot: whether one account,
//   device, or address is moving through the Tier 0 attack sequence (reconnaissance, Kerberoasting,
//   certificate abuse, replication), with stages less than a day apart, which is the AP-5 story.
//
// Logic
//   1. AlertInfo filtered to Defender for Identity alert titles that Microsoft documents in its classic and
//      Defender-format alert lists (both formats appear during Microsoft's transition, so both are listed).
//   2. Each title mapped to a stage.
//   3. AlertEvidence gives each alert its entity keys: accounts (computer accounts ending in $ removed),
//      devices (domain controllers removed through the DomainControllers list), and remote addresses. Each
//      key carries the strong identifiers that Microsoft's custom detection reference lists for mapping an
//      impacted asset: AccountSid, AccountObjectId, and AccountUpn for an account, DeviceName and DeviceId
//      for a device. An address has none: Microsoft maps only assets as impacted entities and lists IP as
//      related evidence, so an address key carries RemoteIP instead.
//   4. Alerts are grouped by entity key, and two alerts at different stages escalate when they fall within
//      ChainWindow of each other, in either order. Each run reads LookbackWindow, which is ChainWindow plus
//      RunInterval, so two stages that fall on either side of a run time are evaluated together in the
//      next run instead of landing in separate runs.
//
// Known false positives
//   - Vulnerability scanners and identity governance tools that enumerate the directory (reconnaissance stage).
//   - Legacy enclave applications (Z-LEGACY) that still request RC4 service tickets during the NTLM and RC4
//     retirement plan (ADR-005).
//   - Entra Connect servers replicate directory changes by design for password hash synchronization;
//     Defender for Identity normally learns this, and DX-06 handles it explicitly.
//   - Shared jump hosts in Z-MGMT where many administrators work can link unrelated alerts by device.
//
// Tuning knobs (let statements below)
//   ChainWindow          time span within which two stages from one entity escalate.
//   RunInterval          the custom detection's frequency, 12 or 24 hours.
//   LookbackWindow       ChainWindow plus RunInterval (Logic 4).
//   DomainControllers    lower-case names of Contoso domain controllers, removed from device evidence so
//                        the target of every alert does not link unrelated alerts.
//   Title lists          extend when Microsoft adds detections; keep each title in exactly one stage.
//   Run frequency: as a custom detection, run this every 12 hours (LookbackWindow 36 hours) or every 24
//   hours (set RunInterval to 24h, LookbackWindow 48 hours), or run it as a hunting query on the same
//   schedule. Microsoft's custom detection reference fixes the lookback for Defender XDR data by
//   frequency: 48 hours at every 12 hours and 30 days at every 24 hours, which hold either LookbackWindow,
//   but 12 hours at every three hours and 4 hours at hourly, which cannot hold ChainWindow and would
//   undercut the escalation that purple-team-plan.md PX-16 expects. Keep the Timestamp filters when saving
//   it: Microsoft advises against Timestamp filters in custom detections except to evaluate a specific
//   subset of the lookback, which is their purpose here. Because LookbackWindow is longer than RunInterval,
//   consecutive runs can return the same chain. Microsoft documents that such duplicates occur when the
//   lookback is longer than the frequency, and that a custom detection that runs twice on an event with the
//   same entities, custom details, and dynamic details creates one alert for both.
//   Alert settings: map the device impacted asset to DeviceName and the account impacted asset to
//   AccountSid (Microsoft allows one column for each entity type; use AccountObjectId or AccountUpn if the
//   lab rows leave AccountSid empty), and RemoteIP as related evidence. Each row fills only the columns for
//   its KeyType.
//
// Response notes
//   Chain reaching replication: treat as a Tier 0 compromise (RR-07). Follow the identity compromise
//   playbook's Tier 0 branch: incident command, sealed AD recovery credentials, two krbtgt password resets
//   separated by at least the maximum ticket lifetime (Microsoft's forest recovery guidance), planned with
//   the directory team and the clinical lead because every site re-authenticates, and no automated
//   disabling of service accounts that clinical systems depend on without a clinical impact check
//   (clinical safety gate, ADR-008; playbook decision point D5).
//
// Validation notes
//   Not run against any tenant. Validate in an isolated lab domain with a Defender for Identity sensor:
//   produce SPN enumeration and a service ticket request for a test service account, then a replication
//   request from a test workstation account that holds no replication rights. Expected telemetry: Defender
//   for Identity alerts in AlertInfo with evidence rows that share the workstation or account. Public
//   library: Atomic Red Team tests mapped to T1558.003 and T1003.006, selected by the operator
//   (purple-team-plan.md exercises PX-04 and PX-05). Certificate stage (exercise PX-16): an anomalous
//   certificate request or issuance on a lab certification authority with a Defender for Identity
//   sensor. Expected telemetry: the sensor's alert in AlertInfo and AlertEvidence; a title in
//   CertificateTitles is placed at stage 3. Alone it returns at Informational, because the native alert
//   carries a single stage. A title missing from CertificateTitles is a tuning finding. If the request
//   appears only in the certification authority's audit log, record the missing rule on those events as
//   a gap (coverage-map.md section 3, item 8). Run boundary: time two of these exercises from the same lab
//   account or workstation so that their stages fall on either side of a scheduled run, less than
//   ChainWindow apart. Expected: the next run returns one row for that entity with ChainedStageCount at 2,
//   at High, or Critical when one stage is replication. Record which identifier columns the lab rows fill
//   (AccountSid, AccountObjectId, AccountUpn, DeviceName, DeviceId) and which impacted asset the alert
//   shows.
//
// References
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-for-identity/alerts-mdi-classic
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-for-identity/alerts-xdr
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-xdr/advanced-hunting-alertevidence-table
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-xdr/custom-detection-rules
//   https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1558/003/
//   https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/006/
//   https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1649/
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-reset-the-krbtgt-password
//
let ChainWindow = 24h;
// The custom detection's frequency: 12h or 24h (Tuning knobs)
let RunInterval = 12h;
// Stages on either side of a run time are evaluated together in the next run (Logic 4)
let LookbackWindow = ChainWindow + RunInterval;
// Lower-case names of every Contoso domain controller (placeholders on a reserved TLD)
let DomainControllers = dynamic(["dc01.corp.contoso.example", "dc02.corp.contoso.example"]);
let ReconTitles = dynamic([
    "Account Enumeration reconnaissance (LDAP)", "Security principal reconnaissance (LDAP)",
    "User and Group membership reconnaissance (SAMR)", "Possible SPN enumeration via LDAP", "Possible SPN enumeration via ADWS",
    "Possible Kerberoasting LDAP reconnaissance", "Suspicious LDAP query", "Possible Active Directory enumeration via ADWS",
    "Possible Active Directory Certificate Services enumeration"]);
let KerberoastTitles = dynamic([
    "Suspected Kerberos SPN exposure", "Possible Kerberoasting attack", "Possible Kerberoasting attack following a suspicious LDAP query",
    "Possible Kerberoasting attack using a stealthy LDAP search", "Suspicious Kerberos SPN request"]);
let CertificateTitles = dynamic([
    "Suspicious Domain Controller certificate request (ESC8)", "Suspicious modifications to the AD CS security permissions/settings",
    "Suspected rogue Kerberos certificate usage", "Suspicious certificate usage over Kerberos protocol (PKINIT)",
    "Suspected certificate enrollment abuse (ESC15)", "Suspicious login attempt using possibly compromised account certificate"]);
let ReplicationTitles = dynamic([
    "Suspected DCSync attack (replication of directory services)", "DCSync attack (replication of directory services)"]);
let Tier0Alerts =
    AlertInfo
    | where Timestamp > ago(LookbackWindow)
    | extend Stage = case(
        Title in~ (ReplicationTitles), "4 Directory replication",
        Title in~ (CertificateTitles), "3 Certificate abuse",
        Title in~ (KerberoastTitles), "2 Kerberoasting",
        Title in~ (ReconTitles), "1 Directory reconnaissance",
        "")
    | where isnotempty(Stage)
    | extend StageRank = toint(substring(Stage, 0, 1))
    | project AlertId, AlertTime = Timestamp, Title, Stage, StageRank;
let EvidenceRows =
    AlertEvidence
    | where Timestamp > ago(LookbackWindow)
    | where AlertId in ((Tier0Alerts | project AlertId));
// One row per alert and entity key, with the key's strong identifiers (Logic 3)
let EntityAlerts =
    union
        (EvidenceRows
        | where isnotempty(AccountName) and not(AccountName endswith "$")
        | extend KeyType = "Account", EntityKey = tolower(AccountName)
        | summarize AccountSid = take_anyif(AccountSid, isnotempty(AccountSid)), AccountObjectId = take_anyif(AccountObjectId, isnotempty(AccountObjectId)),
            AccountUpn = take_anyif(AccountUpn, isnotempty(AccountUpn))
            by AlertId, KeyType, EntityKey),
        (EvidenceRows
        | where isnotempty(DeviceName) and tolower(DeviceName) !in (DomainControllers)
        | extend KeyType = "Device", EntityKey = tolower(DeviceName)
        | summarize DeviceName = take_any(DeviceName), DeviceId = take_anyif(DeviceId, isnotempty(DeviceId))
            by AlertId, KeyType, EntityKey),
        (EvidenceRows
        | where isnotempty(RemoteIP)
        | extend KeyType = "Address", EntityKey = RemoteIP
        | summarize by AlertId, KeyType, EntityKey)
    | join kind=inner Tier0Alerts on AlertId
    | project-away AlertId1;
// For each alert, the stages from the same entity key within ChainWindow of it, in either order (Logic 4)
EntityAlerts
| join kind=inner (EntityAlerts | project KeyType, EntityKey, PeerTime = AlertTime, PeerStage = Stage) on KeyType, EntityKey
| where PeerTime between ((AlertTime - ChainWindow) .. (AlertTime + ChainWindow))
| summarize WindowStages = make_set(PeerStage, 4)
    by KeyType, EntityKey, AlertId, AlertTime, Title, Stage, StageRank, AccountSid, AccountObjectId, AccountUpn, DeviceName, DeviceId
| summarize Timestamp = max(AlertTime), FirstAlert = min(AlertTime), Stages = make_set(Stage, 4), HighestStageRank = max(StageRank),
    ChainedStageCount = max(array_length(WindowStages)), ChainedReplicationAlerts = countif(StageRank == 4 and array_length(WindowStages) >= 2),
    AlertTitles = make_set(Title, 20), AlertIds = make_set(AlertId, 20),
    AccountSid = take_anyif(AccountSid, isnotempty(AccountSid)), AccountObjectId = take_anyif(AccountObjectId, isnotempty(AccountObjectId)),
    AccountUpn = take_anyif(AccountUpn, isnotempty(AccountUpn)), DeviceName = take_anyif(DeviceName, isnotempty(DeviceName)),
    DeviceId = take_anyif(DeviceId, isnotempty(DeviceId))
    by KeyType, EntityKey
| extend RemoteIP = iff(KeyType == "Address", EntityKey, "")
| extend SuggestedSeverity = case(
    ChainedReplicationAlerts > 0, "Critical: chain reaches directory replication",
    ChainedStageCount >= 2, "High: two or more Tier 0 credential-access stages from one entity within ChainWindow",
    HighestStageRank == 4, "High: replication alert, see the native alert",
    "Informational: single stage, the native alert carries it")
| project Timestamp, KeyType, EntityKey, AccountSid, AccountObjectId, AccountUpn, DeviceName, DeviceId, RemoteIP, SuggestedSeverity,
    ChainedStageCount, Stages, HighestStageRank, AlertTitles, AlertIds, FirstAlert
| order by ChainedStageCount desc, Timestamp desc
